Monolingual and multilingual tokenisers

Bertram’s father-in-law’s just made him a cuppa tea.

Why don’t we try and tokenise it a couple of different ways?

BERTT5XLM-RoBERTamT5



from transformers import AutoTokenizer

sentences = {
  "English": "Bertram's father-in-law's just made him a cuppa tea.",
}

tok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained("bert-base-uncased")
for lang, text in sentences.items():
    print(f"{lang}: {tok.tokenize(text)}")

English: ['bertram', "'", 's', 'father', '-', 'in', '-', 'law', "'", 's', 'just', 'made', 'him', 'a', 'cup', '##pa', 'tea', '.']





from transformers import AutoTokenizer

sentences = {
    "English": "Bertram's father-in-law's just made him a cuppa tea.",
}

tok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained("t5-base")
for lang, text in sentences.items():
    print(f"{lang}: {tok.tokenize(text)}")

English: ['▁Bert', 'ram', "'", 's', '▁father', '-', 'in', '-', 'law', "'", 's', '▁just', '▁made', '▁him', '▁', 'a', '▁cu', 'pp', 'a', '▁tea', '.']





from transformers import AutoTokenizer

sentences = {
    "English": "Bertram's father-in-law's just made him a cuppa tea.",
}

tok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained("xlm-roberta-base")
for lang, text in sentences.items():
    print(f"{lang}: {tok.tokenize(text)}")

English: ['▁Bert', 'ram', "'", 's', '▁father', '-', 'in', '-', 'law', "'", 's', '▁just', '▁made', '▁him', '▁a', '▁cu', 'ppa', '▁tea', '.']





from transformers import AutoTokenizer

sentences = {
    "English": "Bertram's father-in-law's just made him a cuppa tea.",
    }

tok = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained("google/mt5-small")
for lang, text in sentences.items():
    print(f"{lang}: {tok.tokenize(text)}")

English: ['▁Bert', 'ram', "'", 's', '▁father', '-', 'in', '-', 'law', "'", 's', '▁just', '▁made', '▁him', '▁', 'a', '▁cu', 'ppa', '▁tea', '.']





Some tokenisers are monolingual/English-centric, others multilingual by design.


As can be seen, subword tokens are sometimes close to morphemes and sometimes not so close
